    <div class="section-title">Axis I: Basic Concepts of Bioenergetics, Thermodynamic Reactions, and Coenzymes</div>

    <!-- Q1 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q1: How is biochemical thermodynamics or "Bioenergetics" precisely defined?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> The study of the velocity of reactions and harmonic configurations of cytoplasmic peptides.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> The study of energy changes and transformations accompanying biochemical reactions in living organisms.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> The mechanical measurement of heat dissipation during direct extracellular glucose degradation.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> The complete sequential arrangement of transfer RNA molecules within mitochondria.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q2 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q2: Biological energy is understood as the capacity to do work. Which of the following forms of energy represents the useful energy capable of doing work at constant temperature and pressure?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Kinetic friction energy.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Latent thermal energy.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Free Energy (Delta G).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Random radiation energy (Entropy).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q3 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q3: What is the biological fate of metabolic energy that is lost or released as "heat energy" during not fully efficient cellular processes?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> It cannot be used to do biochemical work, but it helps maintain the physiological body temperature.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It is captured directly by adenosine diphosphate to produce high-energy enol bonds.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It is completely directed to the mechanisms of the malate-aspartate shuttle.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It acts as a structural catalyst for assembling the cellular nuclear membrane.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q4 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q4: If a metabolic reaction exhibits a negative change in free energy (Delta G), how is this reaction thermodynamically classified?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Endergonic reaction.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Exergonic reaction.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Isometric thermal equilibrium reaction.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Non-spontaneous anabolic reaction.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q5 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q5: Which of the following statements correctly describes an endergonic biochemical reaction?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> It proceeds with a net release of useful free energy and is highly spontaneous.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It possesses an identical concentration of products and reactants at the zero point.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> It requires an input of free energy to proceed, and the change in free energy is positive (Delta G).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It occurs exclusively within the lipid layers of the outer mitochondrial membrane.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q6 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q6: What is the primary physiological source from which the human body continuously derives energy to support its vital and mechanical functions?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> The external reduction of carbon dioxide into nucleic acids.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> The oxidation of major dietary substrates, including carbohydrates, lipids, and proteins.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> The direct hydrolysis of water molecules inside cells.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> The static synthesis of nitrogenous bases via localized physical radiation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q7 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q7: Dehydrogenase enzymes are classified into distinct functional groups based on their hydrogen-carrying coenzymes. Which of the following is NOT one of these groups?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Biotin-linked dehydrogenases.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Nicotinamide-linked dehydrogenases (e.g., NAD).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Flavin-linked dehydrogenases (e.g., FAD).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Lipoic acid-linked dehydrogenases.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q8 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q8: Which molecule represents the typical structural definition of a nicotinamide-linked hydrogen carrier?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> FMN</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Coenzyme Q</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> FAD</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> NAD</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q9 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q9: What is the correct definition of "Redox Potential" in bioenergetics?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> The quantitative tendency or chemical affinity of a given system to gain and accept electrons or hydrogen.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> The number of milligrams of phosphate bound per hour by complex enzymes.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> The precise thickness of the inner mitochondrial lipid membrane.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> The total structural mass of iron-sulfur clusters localized within the matrix.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

