    <!-- Q10 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q10: In the physiological redox spectrum, which two components possess the lowest (most negative) and highest (most positive) redox potential respectively?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Oxygen represents the lowest; Cytochrome c represents the highest.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Succinate represents the lowest; Coenzyme Q represents the highest.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Hydrogen possesses the lowest potential; whereas oxygen possesses the highest redox potential.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Flavin mononucleotide possesses the lowest; Nicotinamide possesses the highest.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q11 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q11: Why is electron transport along the mitochondrial respiratory chain organized in a step-by-step manner rather than a direct, immediate reaction between hydrogen and oxygen?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> The direct reaction requires a permanent inversion in the structure of the mitochondrial double membrane.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> A direct reaction could release a massive amount of energy in an explosive, uncontrolled manner that the cell cannot capture or utilize.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> To prevent the accidental conversion of pure molecular oxygen into dietary glucose units.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Step-by-step regulation prevents the synthesis of metabolic water within the matrix.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q12 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q12: How are high-energy phosphate bonds distinguished from low-energy phosphate bonds in cellular thermodynamics?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> High-energy bonds release a large amount of free energy upon hydrolysis (greater than 7 kcal/mol or approximately 30 kJ/mol).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Low-energy bonds can exist only in situations where oxygen is completely absent.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> High-energy bonds require a specialized mitochondrial nucleic acid for structural stability.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Low-energy bonds contain multiple metallic copper ions to maintain their structural integrity.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q13 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q13: Which of the following compounds is a classic example of a high-energy compound containing a high-energy phosphate bond?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Glucose-6-phosphate.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Glycerol-3-phosphate.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Enol phosphates (e.g., phosphoenolpyruvate) or guanido phosphates (e.g., creatine phosphate).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate intermediate compound.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q14 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q14: What is the specific distinguishing feature of "Substrate-Level Phosphorylation"?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Direct synthesis of ATP molecules from a high-energy metabolic intermediate without the direct involvement or coupling to the electron transport chain.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Mechanical pumping of protons across Complex V using mobile lipid coenzymes.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Production of high-energy chemical bonds catalyzed exclusively by toxic cyanide complexes.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> ATP molecule synthesis that occurs entirely on the outer surface of the outer mitochondrial membrane.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q15 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q15: Where are the multiple protein complexes that constitute the Electron Transport Chain (ETC) anatomically and functionally embedded?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> The soluble fluid portion of the Outer Mitochondrial Membrane (OMM).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Fully dissolved within the aqueous fluid of the matrix.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> The Inner Mitochondrial Membrane (IMM).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> The structural lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-title">Axis III: Detailed Complexes of the Electron Transport Chain (Complex I to IV)</div>

    <!-- Q16 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q16: Which of the following statements comprehensively describes the ultimate and primary function of the Electron Transport Chain (ETC)?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Assembling cytoplasmic pyruvate to convert it directly into lactate units outside the cell.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Transferring electrons from NADH and FADH2 to oxygen to generate water and ATP, while regenerating the oxidized carriers NAD and FAD.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Removing phosphorylation from essential nuclear structures to regulate overall cellular pressure.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Structural cross-linking of cardiolipin molecules in the outer membrane layer.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q17 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q17: Complex I (NADH Dehydrogenase Complex) contains several critical structural components. Which of the following is NOT part of Complex I?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Flavin mononucleotide (FMN).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Iron-sulfur (Fe-S) centers.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Succinate dehydrogenase enzyme.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> The catalytic subunit of the NADH dehydrogenase enzyme.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q18 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q18: Does Complex I participate directly in generating the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Yes, it acts as a proton pump, translocating hydrogen ions (H+) from the matrix to the intermembrane space.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> No, it only channels electrons to Coenzyme Q without pumping any protons.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Yes, but it operates in reverse, translocating protons into the matrix only.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It acts as a proton pump only when molecular oxygen is completely absent.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

