    <!-- Q19 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q19: What unique structural and functional property distinguishes Complex II (Succinate Dehydrogenase) from the other complexes of the respiratory chain (I, III, IV)?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> It is the only complex capable of binding directly to toxic carbon monoxide molecules.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> It does not pump protons across the membrane, and directly links the Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) to the electron transport chain.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It contains specialized copper ions required to neutralize water in the mitochondrial matrix.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It functions as a fully soluble protein residing inside the intermembrane space.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q20 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q20: Which statement provides the precise biochemical description of Coenzyme Q (Ubiquinone)?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> A mobile, lipid-soluble electron carrier located within the lipid bilayer that links Complexes I and II to Complex III.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> A hydrophilic protein that floats in the matrix and transfers phosphate groups to Complex V.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> A structural glycoprotein in the outer membrane that prevents protons from leaking into the cytosol.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> A specialized enzyme that oxidizes cytoplasmic NADH molecules into active FADH2.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q21 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q21: Which respiratory cytochromes specifically compose Complex III (Cytochrome c Reductase)?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cytochrome a and Cytochrome a3.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Cytochrome b and Cytochrome c1 (Cytochrome b & c1).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Cytochrome P450 and Cytochrome b5.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Cytochrome d and structural variants of Cytochrome f.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q22 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q22: During the transfer of electrons through the cytochrome network in the chain, how does the oxidation state of the central iron atom oscillate?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> It alternates between metallic iron and an uncharged gaseous state.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It undergoes irreversible dissociation, releasing free nitrogen atoms into the matrix.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> It continuously shuttles and transitions between the ferric and ferrous states (Fe^3+ \leftrightarrow Fe^2+).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It forms temporary covalent bonds with the adenosine ring structure.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q23 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q23: What is the specific functional mechanism of the mobile peripheral electron carrier Cytochrome c?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> It moves along the outer surface of the inner membrane, transferring electrons from Complex III to Complex IV.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It pumps four protons out of the matrix for every electron it accepts.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It binds directly to succinate to initiate FADH2 oxidation at Complex II.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It acts as an essential channel for the simultaneous transport of protons and phosphate groups.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-title">Axis IV: Complex IV, Complex V, ATP Yield Calculations, and Cellular Respiration Inhibitors</div>

    <!-- Q24 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q24: Complex IV (Cytochrome c Oxidase) represents the final site where molecular oxygen is reduced. What are its specific functional components?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cytochrome b, cytochrome c1, and multiple structural iron-sulfur units.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Cytochrome a, cytochrome a3, and specialized copper centers (Cu_A and Cu_B).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> NADH dehydrogenase enzyme and its tightly bound flavin mononucleotide coenzymes.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Rotor spatial units F_0 and catalytic spherical heads F_1.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q25 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q25: What is the structural configuration and exact location of the two subcomponents constituting Complex V (ATP Synthase)?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> The F_0 part forms a proton channel embedded in the inner membrane, while F_1 represents the catalytic head projecting into the matrix.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> F_0 represents the catalytic head inside the intermembrane space, while F_1 forms a channel in the outer membrane.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Both components are entirely soluble and float freely in the cellular cytosolic environment.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> F_0 binds directly to copper ions in the matrix; while F_1 binds to iron-sulfur clusters in the outer layer.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Q26 -->
    <div class="question-box">
      <div class="question-text">Q26: According to Peter Mitchell's Chemiosmotic Hypothesis, how does proton flow through Complex V lead to ATP synthesis?</div>
      <div class="options-container">
        <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Protons chemically reduce the adenine ring in ADP to form a high-energy carboxyphosphate.</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Protons alter membrane fluidity, causing the static precipitation of mitochondrial glucose.</div>
        <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Protons flow back into the matrix through F_0, driving its rotation and inducing conformational changes in F_1 that bind ADP to inorganic phosphate (P_i).</div>
        <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Protons act as mechanical activators for soluble succinate dehydrogenase enzymes.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

